    "lawCode": "Amd §803, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S261\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to including credits\nearned from a higher education institution as a condition on which the\nmerit board may grant merit time\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1 and 2 amend respectively subparagraph (iv) of paragraph (d)\nof subdivision 1 of section 803 of the correction law, as added by\nsection 7 of chapter 738 of the laws of 2004 and subparagraph (iv) of\nparagraph (d) of subdivision 1 of section 803 of the correction law, as\nadded by section 10-a of chapter 738 of the laws of 2004 to provide that\nan inmate who obtains at least 18 credits in a degree-granting program\nat an accredited higher education institution may be granted a merit\ntime allowance.\n\nSection 3 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder New York State Law, there are two categories of eligibility for\nearly release: 1) Merit Board, which is for people with non-violent\nconvictions, and 2) Limited Time Credit Allowance (LTCA), which is for\npeople with violent convictions. Currently, participation in a college\nprogram is not included as part of the criteria that makes an incarcer-\nated person Merit Board eligible but is included in the criteria that\nmakes one LTCA eligible. As college in prison has been recognized under\none eligibility regime but not the other, this discrepancy calls for\nimmediate reconciliation.\n\nThis lapse in Correction law disproportionately affects women. Of Bard\nPrison Initiative (BPI) students, women are much more likely than men to\nbe eligible for Merit Board rather than LTCA consideration. The people\naffected are those who come to prison having already completed a high\nschool diploma or equivalency (and cannot therefore earn merit time by\ndoing so). These individuals enroll in college, but as they get closer\nto release learn that they must drop their college courses in favor of\nmerit-eligible programs (e.g. vocational training). If college were\nincluded in the Merit Board eligibility criteria, no one would have to\ndecide between dropping out of college and staying in prison longer.\nThis is a false choice, as college has been shown to be one of the most\neffective means to successfully reintegrate someone into society.\n\nAs it stands, this discrepancy in the law affects an estimated 13% of\nfemale BPI students. A student who has completed at least 18 credits in\na degree-granting program at an accredited institution of higher learn-\ning is more likely to continue their education post-release, gain\nemployment, have access to resources and leverage their education\nthrough their relationship with said institution.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: A9543 (Epstein)/ S7633A (Myrie)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nUnknown.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided that the amendments to\nsubparagraph (iv) of paragraph (d) of subdivision 1 of section 803 of\nthe correction law made by section one of this act shall be subject to\nthe expiration and reversion of such section pursuant to section 74 of\nchapter 3 of the laws of 1995, as amended, when upon such date the\nprovisions of section two of this act shall take effect.\n",
